[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-333576"] :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a first-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3}$\n- $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{2}=0.3(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{3}=9(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-334648/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "86b8bfd6-8cdf-5ba6-9701-86a2fc8096ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334648/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "304d8479-26b9-532c-8eb4-750823e6c56c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334648/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "b3588f0c-aad1-5915-a6f2-6c86ce681172" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334648/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "70b683ac-d889-5751-928a-2e3244869cda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334648/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "371d5d0d-0a4c-515c-b2db-00ad19b0f3f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334648/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "afaea038-a4d6-5c43-aaaf-06cbe4b5e728" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334648/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-334648/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-334648/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-334648/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-334648/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-334648/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334648" :question/id #uuid "a0158779-3bb5-5926-932e-57d4963e6b20" :question/math-academy-id "q-334648" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.35,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.35)=0.75.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.005.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.35$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.35}{0.10}=3.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(3.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.75$ to $0.005.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.75}{0.005}=150.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.5)^{p+1} & ≈150 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3.5}⁡(150) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.999… \\\\ p & ≈2.999….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334648/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334648"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334710/field-1/c01be69fe812f7cb27bf7043bbb0bc85bda5ba8878dfb5ae47287b7fc571ee81" :answer/id #uuid "c054062f-958a-5f8b-8d2c-b7b24a5c2a02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.550" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334710/field-1/be2996522c88880b85c94a2df96123acadb7ac9f8da34e41793102bb8efe9a83" :answer/id #uuid "08c848bb-653a-533a-85d4-dfa289c846fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.075" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334710/field-1/b34544a9837d181994c7a132937df1564b327d325fab23b2c613d8bcfe8cb599" :answer/id #uuid "07522058-7c46-5427-85c3-dc577aea7ceb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.375" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334710/field-1/1c3749c3cd74b1bfc2266a575b44a558f14ce2e9c18402bcc68e3a99d24a4157" :answer/id #uuid "40b938c1-d0a2-56e9-97a0-b28d20fdd30c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.057" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334710/field-1/961991a7f93b751281fcba76338cc9ff87be02987f655e77f53c85797d18537d" :answer/id #uuid "5684bcf7-47