ct "q-334710/field-4/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-334710/field-4/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-334710/field-4/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-334710/field-4/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-334710/field-4/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-334710/field-4/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334710" :question/id #uuid "191ac454-645f-542b-be97-450b5f3d1a65" :question/math-academy-id "q-334710" :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}-(Δx)^{6}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.4,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.6) & =6(0.6)^{5}-(0.6)^{6} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.077\\,76-0.046\\,656 \\\\  & =0.466\\,56-0.046\\,656 \\\\  & =0.419\\,904 \\\\  & ≈0.420.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.4) & =6(0.4)^{5}-(0.4)^{6} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.010\\,24-0.004\\,096 \\\\  & =0.061\\,44-0.004\\,096 \\\\  & =0.057\\,344 \\\\  & ≈0.057.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.4.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.4}=1.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={1.5}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.419\\,904$ to $0.057\\,344.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.419\\,904}{0.057\\,344}=7.322\\,54…≈7.323.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1.5}^{p+1} & ≈7.322\\,54… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{1.5}⁡(7.322\\,54…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.910… \\\\ p & ≈3.910….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}-(Δx)^{6}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{5})=O\\,((Δx)^{4+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-334710/field-1" "q-334710/field-2" "q-334710/field-3" "q-334710/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334710"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334761/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "0051b7f1-7fda-58b6-ae60-9ef229dd4508" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334761/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "287d93c6-0cdf-5acd-b44a-b5d41b24e85c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334761/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "a49d378a-b07f-5e09-85f6-5fcc96f7fedf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334761/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "8c4ccffc-c54c-54c0-b429-83f623aa78b8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334761/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "74e26eb5-2461-52e6-9366-65648ba85ab4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334761/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b8421b7e-5b2a-5070-98d2-12fb201b0d03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-334761/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-334761/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-334761/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-334761/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-334761/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-334761/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334761" :question/id #uuid "49d42f68-ce62-5114-8630-2000eca229d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-334761" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7}$\n- $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.5(Δx)^{4}=0.5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{2}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-334761/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334761"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334763/field-1/93e09a05b80f698d016c0968c3fb4ae5bed9fd483754e17f516e5afaf4569f03" :answer/id #uuid "0bd72683-d4b7-5963-a523-5ad25dcde25f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.008" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334763/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8b88f266-70c0-5413-b79d-d7e4231774ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334763/field-1/93e09a05b80f698d016c0968c3fb4ae5bed9fd483754e17f516e5afaf4569f03" :answer-field/choices ["q-334763/field-1/93e09a05b80f698d016c0968c3fb4ae5bed9fd483754e17f516e5afaf4569f03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334763" :question/id #uuid "b4057e0b-c56a-5d6e-a19f-cf1eace4c3c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-334763" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.2$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.2)≈0.064.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.2$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.2}{0.1}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3}={2}^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.1)≈\\frac{0.064}{8}=0.008.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334763/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334763"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
